# Test fixtures are DATA, not source. Their exact bytes — including line
# endings — are what the parser under test is asserted against, so git must
# never rewrite them.
#
# Why this exists: this repo is developed on Windows, where git's autocrlf
# converts LF to CRLF at checkout. `tools/segments/fixtures/*.vtt` were
# committed as LF and arrived as CRLF, which broke a test that constructs a
# CRLF variant from a fixture it assumed was LF (it produced "\r\r\n"). CI runs
# on Linux, so the failure was invisible there and only appeared on a developer
# checkout — the worst combination.
#
# `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion for these paths in both
# directions. Scoped deliberately to fixtures rather than applied repo-wide:
# a blanket normalisation pass on a Windows checkout is its own well-known
# trap, and nothing here needs one.
tools/segments/fixtures/** -text
tools/transcribe/fixtures/** -text
backend/fixtures/** -text

# STATE.md is the cross-session coordination board, and it is a conflict magnet:
# every agent appends a workstream entry to the same "Active workstreams"
# section, so two branches opened the same day almost always collide there.
#
# On 2026-08-16/17 that silently stalled three PRs — #192, #206 and #198 — each
# sitting DIRTY while looking identical to a PR merely waiting on CI, because
# nothing alerts on a conflicted PR. Every resolution was the same: keep both
# entries, since neither supersedes the other.
#
# `merge=union` makes git do exactly that automatically for additive hunks.
#
# The limit, stated because it is real: union is right for append-only files and
# wrong for restructuring. When an entry MOVES (Active -> Completed), union can
# keep both copies rather than the move. That produces a visibly duplicated
# entry, not a silent data loss, and a duplicated heading in a coordination doc
# is a far cheaper failure than three PRs stalling unnoticed. If a restructure
# is ever conflict-heavy, resolve that one by hand.
STATE.md merge=union
